## Custom Metrics

This is the most important part of the system.

Every candidate project should be scored from 0–100.

## Hiring Signal Score — 20 Points

Measures how strongly the project maps to real 2026+ hiring needs.

Signals:

> appears in job descriptions
> maps to actual job tasks
> uses tools employers ask for
> produces interview-discussable work
> aligns to senior/practical expectations
> shows production judgment
> teaches current workflows
> supports role-path readiness

Example:

> Databricks Delta Lake medallion pipeline: 18/20
> Toy CSV calculator: 3/20

## Portfolio Artifact Score — 15 Points

Measures how impressive the final deliverable is.

High score if it produces:

> GitHub repo
> README
> architecture diagram
> tests
> sample outputs
> deploy/run instructions
> dashboard/report
> CI workflow
> monitoring output
> data quality report
> model evaluation report
> cost/security notes
> tradeoff documentation

## Sandbox Feasibility Score — 15 Points

Measures whether Atlas can teach it without requiring dangerous or expensive cloud access.

High score if it can run with:

> Pyodide
> DuckDB-WASM
> local dbt
> mock APIs
> fixture data
> deterministic LLM mocks
> simulated cloud resources
> mock event streams
> local JSON/CSV/Parquet files

Low score if it requires:

> paid cloud infrastructure
> external API keys
> unstable third-party APIs
> long-running compute
> expensive GPU usage
> manual provider setup

## Validation Strength Score — 15 Points

Measures whether Atlas can verify meaningful learner output.

High score if validation can use:

> exact
> structured contains
> signed runtime output
> csv_set_equal
> sql_resultset
> deterministic test harness
> schema validation
> lint checks
> artifact checks
> row-set comparison
> metrics threshold comparison

Low score if it depends mostly on:

> self-attest
> prose-only validation
> contract-shaped metadata
> unverified screenshots
> manual-only review

## Role-Path Coverage Score — 10 Points

Measures whether it fills a catalog gap.

High score if it fills:

> under-covered discipline
> missing difficulty tier
> missing capstone
> missing cloud lane
> missing SQL mastery skill
> missing Python mastery skill
> missing prerequisite bridge
> missing project type

## Cloud / Platform Relevance Score — 10 Points

Measures how well the project maps to important platforms and tools:

> AWS
> Azure
> GCP
> Snowflake
> Databricks
> dbt
> Spark
> Airflow
> Kafka
> Kubernetes
> MLflow
> Bedrock
> Vertex AI
> Azure OpenAI
> Glue
> Athena
> BigQuery
> Delta Lake
> Unity Catalog
> Redshift
> Synapse

## Differentiation Score — 10 Points

Measures whether the project is better than generic online tutorials.

High score if it includes:

> realistic business scenario
> production-style constraints
> testing/evaluation
> failure handling
> cost awareness
> security awareness
> privacy awareness
> architecture tradeoffs
> monitoring/observability
> data quality
> CI/CD
> debugging
> incident-style thinking
> stakeholder requirements

## Build Risk Score — 5 Points

This is reverse-scored. Low risk receives high points.

Risks:

> requires real paid cloud resources
> requires unstable external APIs
> requires unavailable browser runtime
> requires secrets
> is hard to validate
> is too large for one project
> requires unsupported package
> depends on provider UI changes
