> MLflow experiment tracking
> Unity Catalog governance lab
> Databricks Jobs orchestration

Each provider specialist proposes projects but cannot publish them.

## Full 900–1000 Project Scaling Strategy

Atlas should not attempt to generate 900–1000 projects randomly.

It should scale through disciplined waves.

## Discipline-Level Target

Eight disciplines:

> Data Engineering
> AI Engineering
> MLOps Engineering
> Analytics Engineering
> Cloud Data Engineering
> Applied LLM Engineering
> Python Mastery
> SQL Mastery

Long-term target:

> ~120 projects per discipline
> ≈ 960 projects total

Practical premium range:

> 900–1000 premium projects

## Project Distribution per Discipline

Each discipline should have:

> 30–40 beginner projects
> 40–50 intermediate projects
> 30–40 advanced projects
> 5–10 flagship capstones

## Project Wave Strategy

Each wave should generate:

> 8–12 candidates
> 6–8 selected for authoring
> 4–6 published after review
> 2–4 parked or revised

This avoids publishing everything the AI generates.

## Hidden-First Production Rule

Every project wave follows:

> generate candidates
> → score
> → approve selected candidates
> → author hidden projects
> → run audits
> → red-team
> → manual checklist
> → publish only winners

## Quality Gate Before Visibility

A project cannot be visible unless:

> audit:authoring passes
> audit:pedagogy passes
> difficulty is reasonable
> validation is honest
> hints are non-leaky
> scenario is coherent
> portfolio artifact is clear
> no H1/H2 overclaims
> no fake cloud claims
> no placeholder steps
> manual checklist passes

## Preventing Shallow AI-Generated Content

Atlas prevents shallow content through:

> multi-agent review
> custom scoring rubrics
> hidden-first publishing
> manual approval gates
> validation-strength scoring
> portfolio artifact scoring
> hiring-manager scoring
> cloud feasibility scoring
> red-team review
> pedagogy audit
> copy-safety audit
> BC validation audits
> role-path coverage analysis
> duplicate detection

A shallow project is rejected if it:

> does not map to a real job task
> does not produce a portfolio artifact
> cannot be validated
> duplicates existing content
> has vague instructions
> has generic scenario
> has no realistic constraints
> has no failure handling
> has no recruiter value
> has fake cloud integration

## What This Enables

This enables Atlas to:

* scale beyond manual curriculum planning
* identify projects based on market signal
* prevent shallow project generation
* protect hiring relevance
* balance the catalog across disciplines
* preserve validation honesty
* prioritize portfolio-grade work
* build toward 900–1000 premium projects safely

## Why This Matters

This is how Atlas can scale to **900–1000 premium projects** without becoming a pile of shallow AI-generated content.

